\cf0 From The ARES E-Letter for September 16, 2015\
\
==> MAKE AN EMERGENCY COMMUNICATION PLAN\
\
This article, from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ready.gov/make-a-plan"}}{\fldrslt http://www.ready.gov/make-a-plan}}, explains what\
an emergency communication plan is and WHY YOU SHOULD MAKE ONE FOR YOUR\
FAMILY. \'a0It also provides tips and templates on how to make a plan.\
\
\
WHY MAKE A PLAN?\
\
Your family may not be together if a disaster strikes, so it is\
important to think about the following situations and plan just in\
case. Consider the following questions when making a plan:\
\
- How will my family/household get emergency alerts and warnings\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ready.gov/alerts"}}{\fldrslt http://www.ready.gov/alerts}}\
\
- How will my family/household get to safe locations for relevant\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0emergencies?\
\
* How will my family/household get in touch if cell phone, internet,\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0or landline doesn't work?\
\
- How will I let loved ones know I am safe?\
\
- How will family/household get to a meeting place after the emergency?\
\
\
DOWNLOAD AND PRINT A PLAN\
\
Here are PDF templates that you can download, print, and fill out:\
\
- For parents\
\'a0\'a0\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/34330"}}{\fldrslt http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/34330}}\
\
- For kids\
\'a0\'a0\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/34330"}}{\fldrslt http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/34330}}\
\
- For transit commuters\
\'a0\'a0\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/90370"}}{\fldrslt http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/90370}}\
\
- For your wallet\
\'a0\'a0\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/108887"}}{\fldrslt http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/108887}}\
\
- Steps to make a plan\
\'a0\'a0\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/108887"}}{\fldrslt http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/108887}}\
\
- Tips on emergency alerts and warnings\
\'a0\'a0\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/94715"}}{\fldrslt http://www.fema.gov/media-library/assets/documents/94715}}\
\
\
Here are a few easy steps to start your emergency communication plan:\
\
Understand how to receive emergency alerts and warnings. Make sure all\
household members are able to get alerts about an emergency from local\
officials. Check with your local emergency management agency to see\
what is available in your area, and learn more about alerts by visiting:\
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ready.gov/alerts"}}{\fldrslt http://www.ready.gov/alerts}}. Examples of media for alerts include:\
\
\'a0* phone (work, cell, office)\
\'a0* email\
\'a0* social media\
\'a0* medical facilities, doctors, service providers\
\'a0* school\
\
Decide on safe, familiar places where your family can go for protection\
or to reunite. Make sure these locations are accessible for household\
members with disabilities or access and functional needs. If you have\
pets or service animals, think about animal-friendly locations.\
\
Examples of meeting places:\
\
\'a0* In your neighborhood: A mailbox at the end of the driveway, or a\
\'a0\'a0\'a0neighbor's house.\
\
\'a0* Outside of your neighborhood: library, community center, place of\
\'a0\'a0\'a0worship, or family friend's home.\
\
\'a0* Outside of your town or city: home of a relative or family friend.\
\'a0\'a0\'a0Make sure everyone knows the address of the meeting place and discuss\
\'a0\'a0\'a0ways you would get there.\
\
Discuss family/household plans for disasters that may affect your\
area and plan where to go. Plan together in advance so that everyone in\
the household understands where to go during a different type of\
disaster like a hurricane, tornado, or wildfire.\
\
\'a0* Collect information. Create a paper copy of the contact information\
\'a0\'a0\'a0for your family.\
\
\'a0* Identify information and pick an emergency meeting place.\
\
\'a0* Share information. Make sure everyone carries a copy in his or her\
\'a0\'a0\'a0backpack, purse, or wallet. You should also post a copy in a central\
\'a0\'a0\'a0location in your home, such as your refrigerator or family bulletin\
\'a0\'a0\'a0board.\
\
\'a0* Practice your plan. Have regular household meetings to review your\
\'a0\'a0\'a0emergency plans, communication plans and meeting place after a\
\'a0\'a0\'a0disaster, and then practice, just like you would a fire drill.\
}